<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cacamatzin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1483–<a href="/years/1520">1520</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cacamatzin was the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> from 1516 to 1520, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nezahualpilli">Nezahualpilli</a> and nephew of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a>. Chosen with Mexica backing over his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ixtlilxochitl-ii">Ixtlilxochitl II</a>, he was seized by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> in 1520 after plotting resistance to the Spanish. He was killed in Spanish custody in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> in June 1520.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cacamatzin.jpg" alt="Cacamatzin" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manuscript by Fray Bernardino de Sahagún. Illustration by an unknown artist. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cacamatzin,_~1494_-_7-1520,_retrato_an%C3%B3nimo_(siglo_XVI).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
